definition:
    The SI unit of absorbed radiation dose: 1 Gy means one joule of radiation energy deposited per kilogram of material, adopted in 1975 and named after British physicist Louis Harold Gray, a pioneer of radiation dosimetry. It replaced the older rad (1 Gy = 100 rad). Gray measures pure physical energy absorption — to gauge biological harm, sieverts weight each radiation type by its effectiveness.
